WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.010
Limestone consists mainly of calcite and commonly contains fossils.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.850
It can dissolve and reacts with hydrochloric acid.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:12.530
Dolostone consists mainly of dolomite and generally resists dissolution better.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.170
Carbonate can be mixed with clay or quartz, so color alone

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.830
does not distinguish the minerals or establish where the rock formed.

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:24.290
Limestone can form in evaporative lakes and as chemical deposits around

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.790
springs. Porous layered travertine can accumulate in hot or cold springs,

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.470
lakes and caves. Each new layer coats material already present.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.430
The lake pillars and spring deposits are carbonate examples without requiring

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.150
a marine reef at the site.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:43.690
Reef organisms extract calcium carbonate to construct shells and skeletons.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.810
Their remains and lime mud accumulate on shelves.

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:51.390
Waves can break reef material into calcite sand that later becomes

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.730
clastic limestone. Carbonate composition and clastic texture can therefore occur together.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.710
In the deepest sea, carbonate may dissolve instead of being preserved.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.690
Magnesium-bearing fluids can interact with limestone and replace calcite with dolomite.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.690
Replacement may remain incomplete, leaving limestone and dolostone together.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.530
This is a chemical modification of the rock, distinct from moving

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.110
sand grains or simply growing a new reef.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.830
Keep original sediment formation and later mineral replacement separate.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:25.150
In dry settings, carbonate rocks can form resistant cliffs.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:30.110
In wetter conditions, dissolution exploits fractures and bedding, leaving openings, pillars

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.410
and caves. Groundwater carries dissolved carbonate away.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.430
If a cave roof collapses, a sinkhole can form.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.050
Erosion resistance depends on climate and structure, so limestone does not

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.850
always produce the same landform.
